Squaring √2=a/b and multiplying by b² gives 2b²=a².","statement":"a²=2b²."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["square-equality"],"id":"a-even","justification":"The equality makes a² even. If a were odd, a=2k+1 for some integer k, so a²=4k(k+1)+1 would be odd, a contradiction. Thus a is even.","statement":"a is even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["square-equality","a-even"],"id":"b-even","justification":"Write a=2c. Substitution into a²=2b² gives 4c²=2b², hence b²=2c². The same odd-square argument used in a-even shows b is even.","statement":"b is even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["a-even","b-even"],"id":"lowest-terms-contradiction","justification":"If both a and b are even, then 2 is a common divisor, contradicting that a/b is in lowest terms.","statement":"The temporary representation contradicts its lowest-terms condition."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["lowest-terms-contradiction"],"id":"conclude-irrational","justification":"The contradiction discharges the temporary assumption that √2 has a lowest-terms rational representation. Therefore no such representation exists.","statement":"√2 is irrational."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}
